Subject:    context: coerce the V cache type to K on models with no separate V
            cache, instead of refusing to load
Upstream:   NOT FILED, and not going to be. Standing project ruling on outward
            submissions ("Someone else might refile, but not us."). There is no
            issue number to track and nobody is chasing this.
Applies to: TheTom/llama-cpp-turboquant @ fb2cc35ab3507a5aab56fe593620cd96d8f45384
            (src/llama-context.cpp, inside llama_init_from_model)

WHAT IT FIXES
=============
An MLA model (and LLM_ARCH_DEEPSEEK4) has no separate V cache at all: V is a view
of K, so `type_v` describes a buffer that does not exist. Upstream's response to
being handed a `type_k != type_v` pair there is a hard failure —
`LLAMA_LOG_ERROR` plus `return nullptr`, i.e. the model does not load.

That is backwards with respect to how the SAME function treats every other
impossible-or-suboptimal cache configuration a few lines below it:

  * turbo cache types with flash attention DISABLED are not refused — flash
    attention is auto-enabled and a WARN is logged;
  * a quantized V with `FLASH_ATTN_TYPE_AUTO` is not refused — it is promoted to
    ENABLED with an INFO line;
  * flash attention on Grok is not refused — it is forced off with a WARN.

So the established house behavior in `llama_init_from_model` is: coerce the
configuration that cannot work as written, say so, and carry on. The MLA branch
is the one place that hard-fails instead, and it hard-fails the case where the
right answer is unambiguous — there is only one cache, so there is only one type
it can have.

⇒ THIS PATCH REPLACES THE ERROR + `return nullptr` WITH A WARN + `params.type_v =
params.type_k`. K WINS.

WHY K WINS AND NOT V
====================
Two reasons, one mechanical and one about which way a mistake should fail.

  * MECHANICAL: K is the physical buffer and V is the view of it. Honoring
    `type_k` and ignoring `type_v` is the natural implementation of "there is one
    cache", not a policy choice imposed on top of one.
  * DIRECTION OF FAILURE: on this project the operative pair is a high-precision
    K with a compact V (`q8_0` / `turbo3`). Coercing toward K raises V's precision
    and therefore RAISES memory use, which fails loudly and immediately — the
    model does not fit and says so. Coercing toward V would lower K's precision
    silently, and a quality regression nobody is told about is the more expensive
    of the two mistakes. A user who wants the small cache on an MLA model sets
    both types explicitly and gets exactly that, with no warning.

ACCEPTED COST, ON THE RECORD: an MLA model that fits today at `turbo3`/`turbo3`
may fail to fit once a `q8_0` K is in play, because `q8_0` is ~2.7x turbo3 per
element. That is the loud failure above, and it is the trade that was chosen.

WHY IT IS SAFE WHERE IT SITS
============================
  * `params` is a BY-VALUE parameter of `llama_init_from_model` (see its
    signature) — a mutable local. The flash-attention blocks immediately below
    mutate it the same way, so this is not a new kind of write.
  * This runs BEFORE `new llama_context(*model, params)`, which is where the
    memory parameters are built from `type_k`/`type_v`. The coerced value is what
    the context actually gets.
  * The two per-type divisibility checks that follow read `params.type_v`, so
    they now validate the COERCED type — which is the type the cache will really
    have. Checking the discarded one would have been the bug.

WHAT WOULD MAKE IT REMOVABLE (a condition to NOTICE, not an outcome to expect)
=============================================================================
🚨 THIS IS NOT A PATCH WE EXPECT TO CARRY FOREVER, AND THE GUARD SAYS SO IN TWO
DIRECTIONS. What it fixes is a DEFECT in a place upstream is actively editing —
a hard failure where a coercion is available and where the surrounding code
already coerces — so upstream may well arrive at the same conclusion on its own.
The anchor guard in scaffolding/Container.llama-build distinguishes the two
outcomes, and they call for opposite actions:

  * the stock ERROR + `return nullptr` is GONE and our coercion is absent ⇒ READ
    the function at the new ref. If upstream now coerces (or otherwise stopped
    refusing asymmetric types on MLA), DELETE this file and the guard. Do not
    re-derive a hunk for a fix that is already there.
  * the stock code is still present but the hunk fails at fuzz 0 ⇒ ordinary
    drift. RE-DERIVE the hunk at the new sha and update the guard's counts to
    match what you actually found.

VERIFIED 2026-09-07 against ggml-org/llama.cpp master: the branch is still there,
still `LLAMA_LOG_ERROR` + `return nullptr`, byte for byte. Nothing to adopt, and
nothing to wait for.

⚠️ AND IT CAN STOP MATTERING WITHOUT FAILING TO APPLY. If `is_mla()` were removed
or stubbed, this branch would go dead and the patch would apply perfectly while
changing nothing. That is why the guard also asserts the predicate still exists
and that the mutated `params` still reaches the context constructor.

THE BLANK CONTEXT LINES ARE A SINGLE SPACE — DO NOT LET AN EDITOR STRIP THEM
===========================================================================
The hunk's leading and trailing context both include an empty source line, and a
canonical unified diff spells one as a single space. Tools that trim trailing
whitespace turn them into truly empty lines, which makes GNU patch apply this
with fuzz — and the guard runs `patch -F0`, so it would then fail outright. That
is the strictness working as intended; the fix is to restore the spaces, never to
relax the fuzz.

